The inverse of a unit is unique: if $a b equiv a b' equiv 1 mod n$, then
$ b equiv b (a b) equiv b (a b') equiv (b a) b' equiv b'. $
Hence $a^(-1)$ is unambiguous, and cancellation by units follows: if $a$ is a unit and $a b equiv a c mod n$, then $b equiv c mod n$, by multiplying both sides by their inverses.

This is not true in general when the multiplier is not a unit: consider $4 dot 3 equiv 4 dot 8 mod 10$. Certainly $3 equiv.not 8 mod 10$.
